{"name":"June Bug","hex":"#264a48","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"june","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To rush."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/june"},{"term":"bug","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Buginese."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An insect of the order Hemiptera (the “true bugs”).","Any motile small terrestrial invertebrate, especially one that is seen as a pest."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bug"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phyllophaga","blocks":[{"text":"Phyllophaga is a very large genus of New World scarab beetles in the subfamily Melolonthinae. Common names for this genus and many other related genera in the subfamily Melolonthinae are May beetles, June bugs, and July beetles. They range in size from 12 to 35 mm and are blackish or reddish-brown in colour, without prominent markings, and often rather hairy ventrally. These beetles are nocturnal, and are attracted to artificial lights in great numbers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Phyllophaga_spPCCP20040419-4076A2.jpg/330px-Phyllophaga_spPCCP20040419-4076A2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phyllophaga","wikidata":"Q2716763"},{"title":"Cotinis nitida","blocks":[{"text":"Cotinis nitida, commonly known as the green June beetle, June bug or June beetle, is a beetle of the family Scarabaeidae. It is found in the eastern United States and Canada, where it is most abundant in the South. It is sometimes confused with the related southwestern species figeater beetle Cotinis mutabilis, which is less destructive."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Green_june_beetle2.jpg/330px-Green_june_beetle2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotinis_nitida","wikidata":"Q5175388"},{"title":"June","blocks":[{"text":"June is the sixth month of the year in the Julian and Gregorian calendars—the latter the most widely used calendar in the world. Its length is 30 days. June succeeds May and precedes July. This month marks the start of summer in the Northern Hemisphere and contains the summer solstice, which is the day with the most daylight hours. In the Southern Hemisphere, June is the start of winter and contains the winter solstice, the day with the fewest hours of daylight out of the year."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Statue_de_Junon%2C_Louvre%2C_Ma_485%2C_trois_quarts.JPG/330px-Statue_de_Junon%2C_Louvre%2C_Ma_485%2C_trois_quarts.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/June","wikidata":"Q120"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Phyllophaga_commonjunebeetle.jpg/500px-Phyllophaga_commonjunebeetle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Phyllophaga_commonjunebeetle.jpg/500px-Phyllophaga_commonjunebeetle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhyllophaga_commonjunebeetle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/North_American_Junebug.jpg/500px-North_American_Junebug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/North_American_Junebug.jpg/1280px-North_American_Junebug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/North_American_Junebug.jpg/500px-North_American_Junebug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hairy June Bug found in Ohio, USA","link"